.TH "UBERTOOTH\-TX" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-tx\fR \- transmit from an Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-tx \-g PAYLOAD \-a SYNCWORD [\-c MHZ] [\-n COUNT] [\-U <index|serial>] [\-V]
ubertooth\-tx \-l LAP \-u UAP [\-t SECONDS] [\-U <index|serial>] [\-V]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Transmits from an Ubertooth\. There are two transmits, and a command line asks for one of them\.
.P
\fB\-g\fR sends one generic packet: a payload you give in hex, framed by the radio with the sync word \fB\-a\fR gives it\. It is the transmitting half of the generic radio mode, so what it puts on the air is not Bluetooth, and another Ubertooth reads it with \fBubertooth\-dump \-g\fR\. \fBIt is experimental\fR; see THE GENERIC PACKET below\.
.P
\fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR are meant to retransmit symbols captured with ubertooth\-dump toward the piconet that address names\. \fBThat half sends no dump\.\fR Handing the dumped symbols to the board is not written yet, and the usage text says so\. The board does transmit meanwhile: the firmware sends one fixed 16\-byte packet per pair of slots, holding the target's access code, a trailer byte, and seven bytes of filler, and hops along the target piconet's sequence until the run is interrupted or its \fB\-t\fR timeout passes\. So the command puts a signal on the air, carrying the address it was given and nothing from a dump\. Do not rely on it to retransmit a capture\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-g <payload>\fR
transmit one generic packet carrying these bytes, written as hex digits, two per byte and no separators: \fB\-g 0102ff\fR is three bytes\. 1 to 32 bytes, which is one load of the radio's FIFO and all a receiving board reports\. Experimental\.
.TP
\fB\-a <syncword>\fR
the 32\-bit sync word the board frames the generic packet with, 8 hex digits; required with \fB\-g\fR\. The whole of it is written out, because the radio sends all four bytes\.
.TP
\fB\-c <2402\-2480>\fR
the channel the generic packet goes out on, in MHz (default: 2402)\. That range is the Bluetooth band, which is what every tool here tunes, and it is narrower than the radio: the synthesizer reaches 2268 to 2794 MHz and the board will take any of it, so a receiving board can be set outside the band with \fBubertooth\-debug \-w %fsdiv=\fR\. Transmitting outside the band is a question about what you are licensed to radiate rather than about the radio, so this option does not offer it\.
.TP
\fB\-n <1\-65535>\fR
transmit the generic packet this many times (default: 1)\. One request carries the whole burst, so the board sends them back to back; the ceiling is what that request can hold\.
.TP
\fB\-l <LAP>\fR
target LAP (3 bytes / 6 hex digits) for the dump retransmit\.
.TP
\fB\-u <UAP>\fR
target UAP (1 byte / 2 hex digits) for the dump retransmit\.
.TP
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR
timeout for the dump retransmit; 0 means no timeout (default: 0)\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "THE GENERIC PACKET"
Experimental: expect the options and what goes on the air to change\.
.P
The board hands the framing of a generic packet to the radio, so the sync word \fB\-a\fR gives it, plus the framing the firmware programs, is the whole description of what goes out: 6 preamble bytes of \fB01010101\fR, the whole 32\-bit sync word, and the radio's CRC, which is GRMDM \fB0x0f71\fR\. A board reading the packet has to be set to the same thing, and with the CRC bit set it can tell a packet with a bit error in it from a good one \-\- this framing has no other check\. A 32\-byte payload is what makes that verdict trustworthy: the receive reads a fixed 32 bytes and the radio answers the CRC question wherever the read stopped, so a shorter payload leaves it meaningless\. ubertooth\-dump(1), THE GENERIC RECEIVE has the detail\. ubertooth(7), GENERIC RADIO MODE lists the registers both ends have to agree on and walks a packet from one board to another; this page covers only what the options do\.
.P
Two things come from the command line and reach the radio for this one packet: the sync word and the channel\. The power is the board's own, as TRANSMIT POWER below says\. Everything else is the configuration already in the radio, which \fBubertooth\-debug \-w\fR writes\.
.P
\fB\-g\fR stops at 32 bytes, which is one load of the radio's FIFO\. The request itself carries up to 50, but a longer payload is written to the radio a FIFO at a time, each piece after the radio has sent the last, and what that leaves on the air has not been observed: an Ubertooth receiver reads one FIFO's worth per sync word, so it cannot tell one packet from two\. 32 bytes is also all a receiving board reports, and a shorter payload is followed in its record by whatever was next in the radio's FIFO\.
.P
\fB\-n\fR is carried in the one request, so the board sends the burst back to back without the host saying anything more\. That is not only faster \-\- about 1300 packets a second against 440 for a request per packet \-\- it is what makes a burst arrive at all: the board answers no USB request while a packet is going out, so a host sending a request per packet as fast as it can loses the ones that land in those windows\. Measured on two boards, a burst of 30 sent that way had 15 come back as transfer errors and 15 packets reached the air; one request for 2000 packets put 2000 on the air in 1\.6 seconds and a board set to the same framing read all 2000\.
.P
The command waits for the burst to finish, so it ends when the air is clear\. What it waits on is the board leaving the generic transmit mode, which it cannot tell from another command stopping the burst: a \fBubertooth\-util \-S\fR from elsewhere ends this command as a finished burst would\. While it runs the board reports the generic transmit as its mode, which is what \fBubertooth\-util \-w\fR shows and what this waits on\. Ctrl\-C stops the burst: the board takes a stop between packets, and what has not gone out is not sent\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Both transmits use the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leave it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level transmits at 7 (0 dBm), the CC2400's reset value\. The generic packet carries that level in its request, because the level belongs to the one packet, and the board puts its own back once the packet is out\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.P
Two boards a few centimeters apart at level 7 are too loud for each other: a receiver reads nothing from them\. For a test over a short path, turn the transmitting board down to 0 or 1, or put an attenuator in front of the receiving one\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-tx\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: sync word must be 8 hex digits (got cb87)\fR or \fBerror: a generic payload must be hex digits, two per byte, 1 to 50 bytes (got 010)\fR\.
.P
A command line has to name one of the two transmits, and a command line that names neither is refused before any device is opened, so the message never depends on which boards are attached: \fBerror: nothing to do: give \-g to transmit a generic packet, or \-l and \-u to retransmit to a piconet\fR\.
.P
The two cannot be combined, since a generic packet is framed by the sync word \fB\-a\fR gives it and carries no piconet address, and it ends when the packets \fB\-n\fR asked for have gone out rather than after a timeout: \fBerror: \-g cannot be combined with \-l; a generic packet carries no piconet address\fR, and the same for \fB\-u\fR and for \fB\-t\fR\.
.P
Each half needs its own options, and nothing else:
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-g\fR needs \fB\-a\fR, because the board has no sync word of its own and the sync word is the whole of what a receiving board has to be set to: \fBerror: \-g needs \-a <syncword>: the board frames the generic packet with it\fR\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-n\fR belong to the generic packet, so without \fB\-g\fR they ask for nothing: \fBerror: \-a, \-c and \-n are only used with \-g, which transmits a generic packet\fR\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
the dump retransmit needs both halves of the target address: \fBerror: \-l <LAP> and \-u <UAP> are both required\fR\. Half an address reads the same whether or not one of the generic packet's options was written beside it\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
What \fBubertooth\-tx\fR does happens over the air, so it writes no result: everything it prints goes to standard error, and standard output stays empty\. A generic transmit prints nothing at all when it works\. Each error is one line starting \fBerror:\fR\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\. A bare \fBubertooth\-tx\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, after a generic transmit has handed over every packet it was asked for, and after a dump retransmit is interrupted (Ctrl\-C) or its \fB\-t\fR timeout passes\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take and when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened\.
.P
A run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board exits 0 as well: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\. Ctrl\-C during a generic transmit exits 0 too, with the packets not yet handed over not sent\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses a request the transmit needs: the power level a generic packet carries, or a generic packet itself, or the Bluetooth address the dump retransmit sends\. libubertooth reports the refusal and says which request it was\. A \fB\-n\fR run that fails part\-way also says how far it got, so a reader can tell one packet sent from ninety\-nine: \fBerror: the board took 7 of 100 packets\fR\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses the request that ends the dump retransmit: it is left sending, and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
1 as well when a board never reports its burst finished, which reads \fBerror: the board was still transmitting after 100 packets' worth of time\fR: the packets may or may not have gone out, and that board is still in the generic transmit mode\.
.P
A generic transmit stopped by Ctrl\-C exits 0 and says \fBnote: the transmit was stopped part way through 100 packets\fR\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
